course: dictionary
lesson: 4809
title: Humpback: one word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and humpback is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. «Humpbacks sing long songs as they swim.» Here the word means a whale, because songs and swimming belong to animals, not to bridges.
explain: A humpback bridge is a small bridge that rises steeply in the middle, like a hump. «The car slowed down to cross the humpback bridge.» The clue is the word bridge, and the steep rise tells you the shape. Try your meaning in the sentence: a bridge that rises like a hump still makes sense.
explain: Be careful with the third meaning. Long ago, humpback was used for a person with a curved, bent-over back, but it can be unkind. Today we say a person has a curved spine. When you meet an old word in a book, check the context and think about how people speak now.
fact: A humpback, or humpback whale, is a very large whale with long flippers.
fact: Humpbacks are famous for their songs and for leaping out of the water.
fact: «Humpback» can also be an adjective, as in «humpback whale» and «humpback bridge».
fact: Humpbacks have the longest flippers of any whale, about a third of their body length.
fact: Humpbacks can migrate thousands of kilometres, and most whaling was banned in 1986, so they have recovered a lot.
exercise: «Humpbacks sing long songs as they swim.» What is a humpback in this sentence? | A person with a bent back | A small fish that lives in shoals | A bridge with a steep middle | *A very large whale with long flippers
exercise: «The car slowed down to cross the humpback bridge.» What shape is the humpback bridge? | *Rising steeply in the middle, like a hump | Made of glass and very thin | Flat and wide like a road | Hollow underneath like a tunnel
exercise: Old stories call a character a humpback. Why do modern writers avoid this use? | Because it means a small bridge | Because it is a kind of song | *Because it can be unkind | Because it means a very fast whale
test: «Humpback whales work together to blow a spiral net of bubbles.» What do the bubbles do? | Carry the whale up to the surface | Make the water warm | *Trap a shoal of fish | Hide the whales from boats
test: What does «a pod of humpbacks» mean? | A small hill with a steep side | A row of bridges over a river | *A group of humpback whales | A shoal of krill
exam: «Humpbacks have no teeth but baleen, rows of bristly plates in the mouth.» What does baleen do? | Rises steeply like a bridge | Sings long songs to other whales | Carries the whale to the surface | *Sieves tiny krill and small fish out of the water
